[Music]  could jetpacks like this ever be used to  help us get around for decades jetpacks  only existed in films like thunderball  or the rocketeer but in the past few  years a handful of inventors around the  world have tried to turn the idea into a  reality one of those companies is  startup gravity industries that have  developed this iron man-like jet pack  but since it became a reality the jet  pack has kind of been a solution in  search of a problem and has only really  been used for entertainment at stunt  shows now however military and medical  professionals have begun exploring  whether a jetpack could change how  efficiently they get from a to b  it got me wondering just how far jetpack  technology could take us so that's why  i'm here at the goodwood motor circuit  in the back of this land rover being  taken to gravity's rig where they're  gonna strap me in and i'm gonna get to  experience this thing for myself  let's go  okay first a little history about  gravity industries jetpack the company  was founded in 2017 by inventor richard  browning who self-funded the design and  build of a prototype jetpack simply to  see if it was possible since then  richard says he's received over 750 000  in investment and has been flying around  in his jet fuel-powered suit  entertaining crowds breaking some speed  records and occasionally falling into  the water to help prepare me for my  flight and tell me a little bit more  about where jetpack technology is headed  richard dropped in for a little chat  he likes to make an entrance richard  welcome  we've actually started to uncover real  practical applications of this paramedic  response and special forces mobility are  just two of the examples gravity  recently conducted tests in the north of  england to see how efficiently a first  responder could reach the summit of a  mountain if using a jet pack the jet  pack would be used to get immediate  medical attention to a casualty but  couldn't be used to get them down from  the mountain it takes 70 minutes for the  mountain rescue people so over an hour  for the mountain rescue people to walk  to the top we managed to summit that  mountain in three and a half minutes and  the top was completely shrouded in fog  so no helicopter could have got to you  either gravity has also been working  with the uk's naval forces to test  whether the jet pack could be used as a  better way to transfer troops from one  ship to another richard said it takes  around three minutes with helicopters  and ropes but he said the jet pack took  16 seconds and it's not just emergency  services in the uk that are exploring  this technology american firm jetpack  aviation told me that they had sold  jetpacks to a south east asian  government for military use now until  recently the only people who could  really fly gravity's jetsuit were either  richard or his employees that means that  neither the navy nor the paramedics have  actively used this technology yet but  we've actually now trained up real  paramedics and hopefully in the summer  we're going to have them actually on  standby ready to  respond hopefully to the first real-life  casualty so how easy is it to fly a jet  pack there's only one way to find out  let's get you in yeah let's do it  getting rigged into this thing was not a  comfortable process give yourself a lot  of us  that looks great the straps had to be  super tight and the pack was weighed  down with a lot of fuel which was still  only enough to give me around three  minutes of flight i was told i'd be able  to control the thrust by pressing this  button here but a safety supervisor  would control how much power was going  to the engines technically even with the  weight of the fuel the suit is so light  and wingless that it's exempt from many  aviation authority regulations so no  need to register my flight plan so um  let's go flying now richard tells me the  suit can comfortably cruise at up to 60  miles an hour i'm walking up here i did  kind of have some hopes of looking at  least a little bit like iron man  one  but that wasn't the case  at all and yes i am fully aware how  ridiculous i look i was initially  started off on low power but after a few  moments i started to get a feel for how  the jets were pushing my body into the  air so i had one quick go at hovering  and just as i was starting to get a feel  for it  we'd have to stop there okay low on fuel  three minutes of bouncing around had  burned through my pack's fuel supply my  flight experience if you can call it  that was finished as were my ironman  ambitions richard told me i did really  well i don't believe him i mean it's the  most expensive baby bouncer i've ever  been in  so where is jetpack technology headed  for now gravity says its main revenue  stream will continue to be through  entertainment they'll do jetpack shows  offer people paid flying experiences and  plan on launching a jetpack racing  series in 2023  but could this technology find even more  uses well richard says for now it's  unlikely that a jet pack will ever  become your daily driver and having  flown one i can see why are you going to  go to the shops pick the kids ups in  school or go to commute in a thousand  horsepower jetsuit it's as practical as  taking a formula one car the company has  tried making the jetpack electric which  could mean a quieter less flammable suit  but heavy batteries meant it could only  fly for 10 seconds however richard told  me new people were continuing to  approach him with new potential uses for  this tech so we we deliberately are on  this upward spiral with an enormous  community of people in many different  sectors so until jetpack technology  becomes cheaper lighter and more fuel  efficient it's likely it's going to  struggle to get off the ground just like  me  [Music]

